ntrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nWe need to evaluate criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nWe need to consider strengths and concerns.\n\nCandidate A: quick correct decisions (good decision quality), calm in crisis (stress resilience), understands complicated technology (technical competence), but difficulty communicating ideas (crew cooperation, professional communication), egocentric (crew cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So mixed.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress resilience), good at assessing weather (technical competence), excellent computer skills (technical), but weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation, professional communication). So concerns.\n\nCandidate C: anticipates dangerous situations (stress resilience, decision quality), sees complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (crew cooperation, responsibility), but regarded as show-off (crew cooperation), not open to new ideas (adaptability). So concerns.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), concentrates very well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical, decision), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but hot temper (crew cooperation, professional communication), regarded as know-it-all (crew cooperation). So concerns.\n\nWe need to start discussion. Perhaps pick candidate C as leading due to strong anticipation and leadership, but note concerns. Or candidate B for reliability but communication issues. Which is strongest overall? Let's weigh.\n\nC has strong anticipation, complex connections, leadership, spatial vision. But show-off and not open to new ideas could affect crew cooperation and adaptability. D has strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but hot temper and know-it-all.\n\nA has quick decisions, calm, technical, but egocentric and poor communication, not willing to learn.\n\nB has conscientiousness, stress handling, but nasty remarks and weak memory.\n\nWhich candidate best fits overall? Possibly C, as anticipation and leadership are critical for long-distance flights, and the concerns can be mitigated with training. But we need to argue.\n\nWe'll start with recommending Candidate C, citing strengths and acknowledging concerns, and suggest we need to discuss others.\n\nThus public message: state vote for Candidate C, reference that we are starting discussion, mention strengths (anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, leadership) and concerns (show-off, not open to new ideas). Implication: C currently strongest but we need to compare with others.\n\nProceed."}
